{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1788645556822760001,"compiled_state":{"alias":"durational-balsamic-cockatiel","assignee":null,"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":1,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"941881b8add0512d9708fc9ecf9ff126873013ee568fd919c93bea93fb1b81e2","event_uuid":"409eb430-992d-44a2-9ed6-c7a6ae21326b","position":{"commit_sha":"674d1f18988d6260cb983ea78f4b1e56df865e73","position":"1788640418453988012-409eb430-992d-44a2-9ed6-c7a6ae21326b"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[],"created_at":1788640418453988012,"creation_channel":"mcp","deps":[],"description":"Measured while proving the MCP OOM root cause on witted-invisible-roan (9ea3-7d07-ea55-4496). Not a memory defect — a latency one — but it lands on the same ~60s MCP client deadline whose ambiguous timeout causes duplicate billed gate runs (bug d80d-7be7-1c0a-4231), so it is filed rather than left as an aside.\n\n## Observed\n\nAgainst a local `rebar-mcp` (Streamable-HTTP, readonly) over a full clone of this store (8,879 ticket dirs):\n\n| condition | `show_ticket` latency |\n|---|---|\n| server idle | **41.7s**, then 39.9s warm |\n| while 8 concurrent `ready_tickets` are in flight | **80.5s** |\n\nFor comparison, a whole-store `ready_tickets` compile on the same server is 16.3s. A single-ticket read is therefore ~2.5x more expensive than compiling the entire store.\n\n## Mechanism\n\n`resolve_ticket_id` falls through to `_scan_alias` whenever no prebuilt `alias_index=` is supplied (`src/rebar/_ids.py:458-460`). `_scan_alias` (`src/rebar/_ids.py:312`) does `os.listdir(tracker)` and then calls `_effective_alias_for_dir` for EVERY ticket directory, each of which does its own `listdir` plus a `json.load` of that ticket's CREATE/SNAPSHOT event.\n\nSo resolving one id costs a full-store directory walk and ~8,900 JSON parses. The only caller that supplies a prebuilt index is `commit_impact.py:211/233`, so essentially every MCP tool call that names a ticket pays this — and project policy is to WRITE aliases, which is exactly the input that takes the scan path.\n\n## Why it matters beyond latency\n\n1. The MCP client deadline is ~60s. A 40s idle floor means an ordinary `show_ticket` is already inside the same order of magnitude as the timeout, and 80s under a concurrent read burst is over it. A `-32001` timeout is the ambiguous non-signal that provokes duplicate, double-billed gate re-runs (`AGENTS.md`, \"Long gate ops over MCP\").\n2. It is the dominant allocator churn on the read path (tens of MB of parsed dicts per resolution), feeding the same RSS high-water mechanism proven on 9ea3.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: `show_ticket` on an idle server against a store of this size completes in a small fraction of its current 40s, measured before and after with the same harness.\n- [ ] AC2: id resolution no longer parses every ticket's CREATE/SNAPSHOT to resolve one id — verified by counting `json.load` calls (or file opens) during a single resolution, before and after.\n- [ ] AC3: resolution semantics are unchanged across every accepted id form — alias, full canonical id, two-quad short id, legacy single-quad, and Jira key — including the ambiguity and not-found paths, proven by a differential test over the real store comparing old and new resolution for a large sample of ids.\n- [ ] AC4: a held-out regression test fails before the fix and returns to failing when the fix is perturbed.\n\n## Notes\n\nThe natural direction is an alias index built once per store revision rather than per call, but the resolver has ordering/shadowing rules (`_effective_alias_for_dir` exists because an alias can be re-pointed), so AC3's differential check over the real store is the load-bearing part, not the index itself.","env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":[],"ticket_id":"8ee2-cdf6-9de5-4fcd","ticket_type":"bug","title":"Every ticket-id resolution scans and parses the whole store, so show_ticket takes 40s idle","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["409eb430-992d-44a2-9ed6-c7a6ae21326b"]},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1788645556822760001,"uuid":"1beeea1c-c916-4f37-97ce-3fed3003a00b"}